CAT = "s6Accounts"; SEG = "ACC"
Q = [
('A non-discretionary fee-based account usually charges the customer:',
 'A percentage of assets', ['A load on each purchase', 'Nothing on cash balances', 'Only a closing fee'],
 'Fee-based accounts charge a yearly fee based on account assets instead of a commission per trade. They can cost more than commissions for a customer who rarely trades.'),
('In a prime brokerage arrangement, the customer:',
 'Clears all trades through one firm', ['Must execute at one firm only', 'Keeps its assets at each executing broker', 'Pays no clearing or custody fees'],
 'A prime broker clears and holds the assets while the customer, usually an institution, sends orders to several executing brokers that settle through the prime broker.'),
('In a community property state, securities a married couple buys during the marriage are generally owned:',
 'Equally by both spouses', ['By the higher earner', 'By the named owner', 'In shares the firm sets'],
 'Community property states treat most assets acquired during marriage as owned equally by both spouses, whichever name is on the account.'),
('Opening an account for an unincorporated club or association requires documents showing:',
 'Who may act for the group', ['Each member\'s tax return', 'A state corporate charter', 'A court appointment order'],
 'An unincorporated association is not a corporation, so the firm needs its governing papers or a resolution naming the officers authorized to transact.'),
('A partnership account requires the partnership agreement, mainly to show:',
 'Which partners may trade', ['Each partner\'s credit score', 'The firm\'s profit last year', 'Partners\' home values'],
 'The agreement shows who has authority to enter orders and withdraw assets for the partnership. Rule 4512 requires the names of persons authorized to transact for an entity.'),
('A 457(b) deferred compensation plan is offered mainly by:',
 'State and local governments', ['Publicly traded corporations', 'Sole proprietors', 'Federally chartered credit unions'],
 'Section 457(b) plans are for employees of state and local governments and certain tax-exempt organizations, letting them defer part of their pay before tax.'),
('A defined benefit pension plan promises each participant:',
 'A set retirement income', ['A set yearly contribution', 'Whatever the account earns', 'A share of company profits'],
 'A defined benefit plan promises a retirement benefit, often based on pay and years of service. A defined contribution plan fixes contributions instead and pays what the account is worth.'),
('In a profit-sharing plan, the employer\'s contributions:',
 'May vary from year to year', ['Must equal the prior year\'s', 'Come out of employee pay', 'Are fixed by ERISA'],
 'Profit-sharing plans let the employer decide each year how much to contribute, if anything, following the plan\'s allocation formula.'),
('An employee stock purchase plan typically lets workers buy company stock:',
 'At a discount through payroll', ['With a guaranteed rate of return', 'Only after retirement', 'Completely free of market risk'],
 'An ESPP lets employees buy shares through payroll deductions, often at a discount to market. The shares still carry full market risk.'),
('A 403(b) plan for public school employees may invest in:',
 'Annuities and mutual funds', ['Individual stocks and bonds only', 'Collectibles and art', 'Real estate directly'],
 'Section 403(b) limits plan assets to annuity contracts and custodial accounts holding mutual fund shares, which is why variable annuities and funds are common in these plans.'),
('A direct rollover from a 401(k) to an IRA avoids:',
 'Mandatory 20% withholding', ['All future income tax', 'The 10% early penalty forever', 'Required minimum distributions'],
 'When plan money is paid to the participant, the plan must withhold 20% for tax. Sending it trustee to trustee in a direct rollover avoids that withholding.'),
('Moving IRA assets directly between custodians in a trustee-to-trustee transfer:',
 'Has no once-a-year limit', ['Is taxed as a withdrawal', 'Must finish in 60 days', 'Triggers a 10% penalty'],
 'The IRS one-rollover-per-year rule applies to 60-day rollovers the owner handles, not to direct transfers between custodians.'),
('A surviving spouse who inherits an IRA may:',
 'Treat it as her own IRA', ['Only take a lump sum', 'Never roll it over', 'Keep it only five years'],
 'A spouse beneficiary has the unique choice of treating the IRA as her own, delaying distributions as an owner would. Other beneficiaries cannot.'),
('Most non-spouse beneficiaries of an IRA inherited after 2019 must empty it within:',
 '10 years', ['5 years', '15 years', '20 years'],
 'The SECURE Act requires most designated beneficiaries who are not spouses or other eligible designated beneficiaries to distribute the account by the end of the tenth year after death.'),
('Under Rule 4515, a change to the account name on a customer order before execution must be:',
 'Authorized and documented', ['Approved by the customer only', 'Reported to FINRA in 30 days', 'Shown on the next statement'],
 'Rule 4515 requires an authorized person under the firm\'s procedures to approve any change in account name or designation, with the essential facts documented in writing.'),
('Under Rule 4514, a firm drafting a check on a customer\'s bank account needs the customer\'s:',
 'Express written authorization', ['Verbal approval by phone', 'Spouse\'s written consent', 'Notarized power of attorney form'],
 'Rule 4514 bars obtaining a check or draft on a customer\'s bank account without express written authorization, which the customer\'s signature on the instrument can provide.'),
]
